\section{How it works}{

The clear-sky model is ESRA, as implemented by GRASS GIS \code{r.sun}
(Hofierka and Suri 2002). Two components are computed and can be returned
separately:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Beam} - direct sun. At each time step the cell is lit only if the sun
clears its terrain horizon \emph{and} the surface faces the sun. The energy
arriving is the direct-normal irradiance
\code{G0 * exp(-0.8662 * linke * m * dR(m))} - Beer-Lambert attenuation over a
Rayleigh atmosphere - times the cosine of the incidence angle on the
tilted surface. The air mass \code{m} is Kasten and Young's, corrected for
refraction and for the air pressure at \strong{the cell's own elevation}, which
is worth several percent of the beam in mountains.
\item \strong{Diffuse} - scattered skylight. Horizontal diffuse irradiance depends
only on sun altitude and turbidity, so the per-cell part is just the share
of the sky dome that cell can see: exactly what \code{\link[=rvt_sky_illumination]{rvt_sky_illumination()}}
reports, with flat open ground at 1. Diffuse is \emph{not} gated by the shadow
test - ground the sun cannot reach is still lit by the sky.
}

Everything else - the date range, \code{time_step}, \code{reach} and its
multi-resolution levels - behaves exactly as in \code{\link[=rvt_daylight]{rvt_daylight()}}, and the
cost is the same: one horizon search per cell, with the sun positions nearly
free on top.
}

\section{On a canopy model}{

Run on a DSM this gives the light regime of the canopy surface and of gaps,
with three limits worth knowing:
\itemize{
\item \strong{The canopy is opaque.} Real foliage transmits and scatters, so a closed
forest floor receives a few percent of open-sky radiation rather than the
zero modelled here. Read the result as the geometric light regime, a lower
bound.
\item \strong{NoData does not block.} Holes in the surface let light through, which
overestimates it wherever those holes are really canopy.
\item \strong{Normals are noisy at crown edges}, where the 3x3 derivative sees a
near-vertical facet. Gap floors are flat and unaffected.
\item \strong{Set \code{pyramid_method = "q3"}} if \code{reach} is long enough to build coarse
levels. The default \code{"max"} takes the tallest crown in each coarse block
and treats it as solid, which over-states shading on a surface that is all
peaks; see \link{rvt_reach}.
}

A gap also has to be wider than intuition suggests: the sun reaches at most
\code{90 - latitude + 23.4} degrees, so a circular gap needs a radius of about
\code{height / tan()} of that just to catch midsummer noon - at 53 degrees north,
0.57 times the canopy height, and below that the direct beam is exactly zero
all year.
}

\section{This is potential, not actual, radiation}{

There are no clouds in this model. It answers "how much does this terrain
allow", not "how much did this slope receive last July". Every tool of this
kind does the same unless fed measured radiation data, and the usual way to
use the output is as a relative surface - which slopes and hollows are
favoured - or multiplied by a measured clear-sky index for the region.

Reflected radiation from surrounding slopes is not included either.
}

\section{Tuning}{

\itemize{
\item \code{linke} is the Linke turbidity factor, the atmosphere's haziness at air
mass 2, and the one atmospheric parameter. Roughly: \strong{2} very clear
mountain air, \strong{3} rural to small-town (the default, and what VOSTOK
hardcodes), \strong{4-6} urban or humid summer air. It is tabulated by region
and month in the European Solar Radiation Atlas, so a defensible value can
be looked up rather than guessed.
\item \code{component} picks what comes back. \code{"global"} (default) is beam plus
diffuse. \code{"beam"} and \code{"diffuse"} return one of them, and \code{"all"} returns
a three-band raster in that order - useful because the split is where the
terrain story is: beam collapses in a shaded gorge while diffuse only
falls by the sky it loses.
\item \code{sky_model} sets how the diffuse is spread over the sky dome. \code{"uniform"}
(default) is the isotropic assumption normally used with a clear-sky
model; \code{"overcast"} weights the zenith more heavily.
\item \code{reach} matters more here than for a picture-making metric: a ridge that
takes an hour off the winter sun changes the answer materially, so set it
to the distance that actually shades the site.
}
}

